      "id": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/sep/11/nile-gold-mining-sudan-environmental-disaster-poverty-public-health-emergency-crops-fail-chemicals",
      "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/sep/11/nile-gold-mining-sudan-environmental-disaster-poverty-public-health-emergency-crops-fail-chemicals",
      "title": "[The Guardian Global Development] Curse of the Nile’s gold: how Sudan is losing its fertile fields and ability to produce food",
      "content_html": "<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🌾 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.theguardian.com%2Fglobal-development%2F2026%2Fsep%2F11%2Fnile-gold-mining-sudan-environmental-disaster-poverty-public-health-emergency-crops-fail-chemicals\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🌾 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.theguardian.com%2Fglobal-development%2F2026%2Fsep%2F11%2Fnile-gold-mining-sudan-environmental-disaster-poverty-public-health-emergency-crops-fail-chemicals\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\n<p>An artisanal mining boom driven by war and poverty is causing a public health emergency, as chemicals poison soil, water and society</p><p>In Abu Hamad, on the banks of the Nile, verdant orchards, gardens and fields once produced a bounty of dates, citrus fruits, winter grains and cereals for the towns and cities of eastern Sudan, including Khartoum and Port Sudan.</p><p>Today, the land looks drastically different. Trees have been cut down, agricultural areas cleared, and farms have been replaced by shops and commercial sites. Dotted around the town and its outskirts, more than 700 mounds of grey waste are poisoning the soil, water and air. The mounds are the toxic remnants of artisanal gold mining, a practice that began in about 2008 and has intensified over the past six years.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/sep/11/nile-gold-mining-sudan-environmental-disaster-poverty-public-health-emergency-crops-fail-chemicals\">Continue reading...</a>",
